  "reason": "The records collectively suggest high-strain-rate atomistic deformation studies of compositionally similar HEAs, temperature-dependent softening, and an important role for partial dislocations, stacking-fault energetics, and local FCC/HCP structural relationships. In particular, R6 supplies the MD/high-strain-rate precedent, while R5, R12, and R20 support stacking-fault, HCP, and partial-dislocation behavior in closely related Cantor-type alloys. However, none specifies the later paper's bulk equiatomic FeNiCrMn single-crystal tensile matrix across orientations, temperatures, and rates, nor vacancy-cluster-controlled dislocation nucleation, rate-insensitive yield strength, or the complete ISF/ESF mechanism. Thus the literature points in the right direction but substantial synthesis and a new specific study design would still be required."
